{
  "_about": [
    "Golden cases for the transcript frame: the plaintext of an MLS application message that carries a person's words.",
    "Normative: docs/sealed-messages.md §The frame.",
    "Keys: docs/sealed-messages.md §Keys of a frame.",
    "Pointers: docs/sealed-messages.md §Attachment pointers.",
    "Each case is {name, plaintext, expect}. `plaintext` is the decrypted application message in standard padded base64, so a case can hold bytes that are not text.",
    "`expect.kind` is `envelope` (a readable message), `tooNew` (a later frame version: the reader says an update is needed and never shows the bytes as text) or `unreadable` (anything else).",
    "An `envelope` gives `id`, `text` and `attachments` (in order), and, when the frame carries them, `place`, `replyTo`, `broadcast`, `accessKey`, `mentions`, `everyone` and `expireSeconds`.",
    "An absent key reads as its empty value: `place` null, `replyTo` \"\", `broadcast` false, `accessKey` \"\", `mentions` [], `everyone` false, `expireSeconds` 0.",
    "In `expect`, a pointer and a place use long names (`objectKey` for `o`, `durationMs` for `ms`, `lat` for `la`, and so on); the wire keys are the one- and two-letter ones in the spec.",
    "Cases for event cards and story references are left out: those keys are not specified here.",
    "Object keys and ids in these cases are opaque test strings; a reader does not interpret them beyond the rules cited above.",
    "To replay: base64-decode `plaintext`, read it as a frame, and compare the result with `expect`."
  ],
